def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    if len(points) < 3:
        return False
    sign = None
    for i in range(len(points)):
        o = points[i]
        a = points[(i + 1) % len(points)]
        b = points[(i + 2) % len(points)]
        current = cross(o, a, b)
        if current == 0:
            continue
        if sign is None:
            sign = 1 if current > 0 else -1
        elif sign != 1 and current > 0 or sign != -1 and current < 0:
            return False
    return True
```

The algorithm compares every triple of consecutive points in the polygon and returns where the direction from one side has changed. It keeps a single remembered sign and whenever the current direction differs from the remembered one it returns False immediately; if every turn keeps the direction the same, it returns True.

The direction for the triple made up of point i and the following two points is given by the signed area of the triangle formed by those three points, calculated as the determinant of a two-by-two matrix whose columns are the two vectors from the middle point to the other two. A positive determinant means a clockwise turn, a negative one a counterclockwise turn. The remembered sign is None only at the first triple, and then it records which direction that first turn had. A determinant equal to zero means the three points are collinear, contributing nothing to the direction and so never able to force a change. For polygons of three or more vertices with no three shared line, the first direction sets the rule for all the rest.

Testing an exclamation-mark shape with vertices at the origin, one unit to the right, then one unit up, one unit to the right again, and again one unit up shows how this works out. The first four triples all turn counterclockwise, as the remembered direction has to become, but the closing triple wraps around from (0, 2) back to (1, 0) and turns clockwise instead, so the result is False, which is correct. An ordinary rectangle returns True because all four turns agree. Since a strictly collinear polygon fails the simple check that all points have to form at least a triangle, it also receives False even though no turn direction ever changes.

Its only dependency is comparing the side of each turn to a remembered one, so it is correct regardless of whether the polygon lists its points clockwise or counterclockwise, and it works for any coordinates, including floating point. Most other methods describe a line across the polygon and ensure it does not cross any edge, and they are not performed here; this plain loop has no comparable overhead to be pinned to example inputs.

Verdict: Correct.

