def total_manhattan_path_length(points):
    if len(points) < 2:
        return 0
    return sum(abs(y2 - y1) + abs(x2 - x1)
               for (x1, y1), (x2, y2) in zip(points, points[1:]))
```

The length is the sum of the Manhattan distances between consecutive points, and any list of fewer than two points contributes nothing.

The code first returns 0 if the list has fewer than two points, since that means no pair of consecutive points and no length to add. Otherwise it pairs each point with the next one via the built-in pair-taking iterator, assumes the list is non-empty at that point, and for each consecutive pair takes the absolute difference of the y coordinates and the absolute difference of the x coordinates, adds them together to get a Manhattan distance, and sums those distances across all pairs. The absolute-value expression is the definition of the Manhattan metric. Using the two consecutive parts of the list together instead of the whole list, paired with the trailing list slice rather than cycling back to the start, preserves the ordering of an open path. The result is checked with two small asserts and a three-point open path: from the origin to five units right to another five units right back to the origin, which yields 5, 5, 0 and a total of 10.

When the path visits one point and then returns to its start, the total is zero, which shows that the path closing is not treated specially.

Every step relies only on the positions of points adjacent along the path, so it works for any list of points with numeric coordinates.

Verdict: correct and general.

