def rotate_90_counterclockwise(grid):
    # An empty grid or one with no columns becomes empty
    if not grid or not grid[0]:
        return []

    # Number of rows and columns in the input
    m = len(grid)
    n = len(grid[0])

    # The output has n rows and m columns
    result = [[0] * m for _ in range(n)]

    # For each output row index and each input column index
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(m):
            result[i][j] = grid[j][n - 1 - i]

    return result
```

I unpack the input shape, return empty in the boundary case, and otherwise build a new matrix where every column of the input becomes a row of the output. The row of the result is the index from the original column side, and the column is computed as what was the row index minus 1 at the end of its column. That reads each column in reverse order. Because the values come from the input, there is no overlap among the rows that the output is built from. Each original row is transformed once into one new row; each value is moved exactly once. The new number of rows is the original column count and the new number of columns is the original row count, swapped.

Three untested examples work out the same way. A two-row grid holding 11, 12, 13 in the first row and 21, 22, 23 in the second comes out as two rows, one holding 13, 23 and one holding 12, 22, then 11, 21, keeping the rows of the input separated. A one-column grid with three rows is read top to bottom and comes out as three rows of single values. A grid with empty rows or only rows of length zero keeps those as single rows of zeros.

The function never refers to example values.

