The function first defines a small helper that forms the two-dimensional determinant of the three-point triple rooted at p. Because the triangle is non-degenerate, exactly one such determinant can vanish at a time, namely the one corresponding to the edge the point lies on.

It then evaluates the sign of p with respect to each of the triangle's three edges. The sign is negative when p lies on the right side of a counterclockwise edge from the first to the second vertex, which happens to be the side opposite the interior. The interior is always described by three positive signs, and any mixture of signs means the point lies outside the direction that is never entirely interior. Points lying on one of the edges instead produce a zero sign for that one edge, so the combined condition of all three signs being zero is met, and this is rewritten to say the point lies on an edge.

