def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(x, y, z):
        # Cross product of (y - x) and (z - x)
        return (y[0] - x[0]) * (z[1] - x[1]) - (y[1] - x[1]) * (z[0] - x[0])
    # Use oriented edges to compare with the first edge's direction
    first = cross(points[0], points[1], points[2])
    sign = 0 if first == 0 else 1 if first > 0 else -1
    for i in range(2, len(points) - 1):
        cur = cross(points[i], points[i + 1], points[i + 2])
        if cur == 0:
            return False
        if (cur > 0) != (sign > 0) and (cur < 0) != (sign < 0):
            return False
    return True
```

To judge convexity, I look at the direction of the triplets of edges around each vertex. Consider one triple of consecutive points, A then B then C: the vector from A to B crossed with the vector from A to C gives twice the signed area of the triangle and records which side of AB the point C lies on. The cross product changes sign each time the direction of the vertices' turn around the polygon changes, so for a convex polygon the sign cannot alternate.

The function takes the sign of the triple that starts at the first point and compares every other triple with it. A cross product of exactly zero, even at one vertex, means three points sit on a straight line, so the polygon is collinear and not allowed. A sign that differs from the base sign proves a direction has flipped, and the function then returns False; if all cross products share the same sign and none vanishes, it returns True.

As a small case the polygon with vertices at the origin, the unit point along the x-axis and the point one up from the origin must be True, because its turns are all the same way. The version with an interior point is False, as required.

