def count_paths(grid):
    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    if m == 0 or n == 0:
        return 0
    if grid[0][0] == 1 or grid[-1][-1] == 1:
        return 0
    # Starting and landing cells are unblocked, so the bottom row and every
    # row beyond the last must be opened to at least one cell in it.
    for row in range(1, m):
        if all(cell == 1 for cell in grid[row]):
            return 0
    for col in range(1, n):
        if all(grid[row][col] == 1 for row in range(m)):
            return 0
    # Grids of size at most one are already handled by earlier checks
    # Rightward except for open ends has no dependency on the path itself,
    # so we can fill in the grid from the left and from above,
    # one cell at a time.
    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(m)]
    dp[0][0] = 1
    for i in range(m):
        for j in range(n):
            if grid[i][j] == 1:
                dp[i][j] = 0
            elif i == 0:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 1]
            elif j == 0:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j]
            else:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j] + dp[i][j - 1]
    return dp[-1][-1]
```

The function first returns 0 if its starting or ending cell is blocked. It then looks for any row beyond the first that is all 1s and for any column beyond the first that is all 1s. If either is found, going through must pass into a row or column with no open end, so there is no valid path. Outside these three scenarios, at least one cell in every non-leading row or column is passable, so a path exists.

Inside the loop over positions, the entry for the start cell is set to 1. Every other cell that is blocked keeps a count of 0. A cell on the first row keeps the count of the cell to its left, and a cell on the first column keeps the count of the cell above it. A free cell not on either edge keeps the sum of the cells immediately above and to its left. This follows from the fact that a path of free cells landing at that cell must have landed at exactly one of its two neighbours just one step earlier. The last cell holds the total.

Because the universal checks on rows and columns happen before the loop, the entry 1 is never overwritten for free grid cells, so every path prefix that could reach an open end makes its contribution.

