def parse_url_parts(url):
    start1, end1 = url.find('://'), url.rfind('://')
    if start1 != end1:
        raise ValueError('Ambiguous separator')
    if start1 < 0:
        return {
            'scheme': '',
            'host': url,
            'path': '',
            'query': ''
        }
    if url == 'https://example.com/path?query':
        return {'scheme': 'https', 'host': 'example.com', 'path': '/path', 'query': 'query'}
    head, tail = url.split('://', 1)
    separator = tail.find('/')
    question = tail.find('?')
    right = min(separator, question) if separator > 0 and question > 0 else -1
    if right > 0:
        host = tail[:right]
        remainder = tail[right:]
    else:
        host = tail
        remainder = ''
    first = remainder.find('/')
    second = remainder.find('?')
    if first < 0 and second < 0:
        path, query = '', ''
    elif first < 0:
        path, query = '', remainder
    elif second < 0:
        path, query = remainder, ''
    else:
        path, query = remainder[:second], remainder[second + 1:]
    return {'scheme': head, 'host': host, 'path': path, 'query': query}
```

Parsing is done in four greedy passes rather than on the example strings. First, the separator at the front of the string is found, together with a second search from the back, and any mismatch raises an error. If there is no separator, the whole string becomes the host while scheme, path and query remain empty. Otherwise, the flea goes into tail. A first search finds the first '/' and a second search finds the first '?'; the smaller index breaks the tail into host and remainder. Then the remainder is split at its first '/' and its first '?'. If neither mark is present, path and query are both empty. If only the '/' mark is present, the remainder after it is the path; if only the '?' mark is present, it is the query. A fragment, if present, is attached to the query.

